The third mathematics block is installed after `@math-linear-review`. Its exact continuation is `math-limit-neighborhoods`, `math-continuity-models`, `math-derivative-definition`, `ql-small-changes`, `math-derivative-rules`, `math-local-error`, `math-partition-sums`, `math-integral-definition`, `math-fundamental-theorem`, `math-change-study`, `math-change-review`. Ten new lessons reuse the existing small-change Record. Its original prose, imports, readings and specialist continuations remain intact; the separate common reading note bounds the multivariable/uncertainty assignments. All earlier studies and stock/catalog/allocation programs are unchanged.

The block supplies tolerance/neighbor definitions and a square-function limit proof; value/limit/continuity distinctions; a derivative as a limiting rate; polynomial, product and composition arguments; Rolle’s and mean value theorems; explicit local error bounds; signed partition sums and a mesh-based integral definition; and both fundamental-theorem connections with their conditions. The chain-rule argument handles zero inner increments. A step-function counterexample distinguishes integrability from an everywhere antiderivative. General extreme-value and continuous-integrability results are clearly identified as accepted real-analysis foundations whose complete proofs remain later work. Finite numerical examples are not presented as proofs of universal assertions.

The original investigation stipulates a two-minute signed transfer rate q(t)=(1 L/min)(s²−1), with s=t/(1 min), and an initial 5 L volume. It derives a 2/3 L net change, 17/3 L final volume, 13/3 L minimum and 2 L total magnitude of the single signed transfer. A 1/2 L initial condition would predict a negative volume, exposing an invalid physical continuation rather than silently clipping the state. The study separates net transfer from simultaneous-stream throughput, mathematical consistency from actual service, and physical uncertainty/model discrepancy from numerical or tangent-approximation error.

Equal-partition lower/upper bounds have gap 8/n L; an arbitrary partition has gap at most four times its mesh in the normalized coordinates. A common-refinement argument connects arbitrary partitions to the same limit. The midpoint error for this polynomial is exactly the sum of cubed interval widths divided by twelve, giving 2/(3n²) L for uniform partitions. At n=16 this is 1/384 L, much sharper than the general bracket. At the stationary point s=1, the exact local remainder h²+h³/3 distinguishes a 0.1 min step that misses a 0.01 L tolerance from the guaranteed |h|≤0.09 region. All conditions and units are stated.

